1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,060
Meet Dana.

2
00:00:02,060 --> 00:00:03,785
She runs a shift at a warehouse

3
00:00:03,785 --> 00:00:04,960
in Ohio.

4
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:05,212
This spring,

5
00:00:05,212 --> 00:00:05,890
the robots arrived.

6
00:00:05,890 --> 00:00:07,510
They don't take a salary.

7
00:00:07,510 --> 00:00:10,370
They don't take vacations.

8
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:12,370
They never ask for a raise.

9
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:14,028
And the warehouse is making more money

10
00:00:14,028 --> 00:00:15,080
than ever.

11
00:00:15,090 --> 00:00:16,930
So Dana has a question.

12
00:00:16,930 --> 00:00:18,430
If the robots do the work,

13
00:00:18,430 --> 00:00:19,980
who gets the money?

14
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:21,260
Here's a clue.

15
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:22,380
In America,

16
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:24,193
the share of business output paid to

17
00:00:24,193 --> 00:00:26,006
workers has just hit the lowest level

18
00:00:26,006 --> 00:00:27,150
on record.

19
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:29,254
So the real question about AI may

20
00:00:29,254 --> 00:00:29,931
not be,

21
00:00:29,931 --> 00:00:31,570
will I keep my job?

22
00:00:31,570 --> 00:00:32,357
It may be,

23
00:00:32,357 --> 00:00:34,867
do I own any of the machines?

24
00:00:35,317 --> 00:00:42,233
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

25
00:00:53,884 --> 00:00:56,595
We followed the robots in Could AI

26
00:00:56,595 --> 00:00:59,576
Make American Factories Cheaper Than

27
00:00:59,576 --> 00:01:00,362
China's,

28
00:01:00,362 --> 00:01:02,531
and in Could AI Bring Europe Back

29
00:01:02,531 --> 00:01:03,834
to Power.

30
00:01:03,834 --> 00:01:05,586
Now let's follow the money,

31
00:01:05,586 --> 00:01:07,074
through Dana's eyes.

32
00:01:07,317 --> 00:01:08,897
Let me draw it.

33
00:01:08,897 --> 00:01:10,852
Picture a hundred-dollar bill that stands

34
00:01:10,852 --> 00:01:13,407
for everything America earns.

35
00:01:13,427 --> 00:01:15,134
A slice goes to the people who

36
00:01:15,134 --> 00:01:15,967
do the work,

37
00:01:15,967 --> 00:01:17,737
as pay and benefits.

38
00:01:17,737 --> 00:01:19,411
A slice goes to the owners,

39
00:01:19,411 --> 00:01:20,617
as profit.

40
00:01:20,617 --> 00:01:23,285
The rest covers machines wearing out,

41
00:01:23,285 --> 00:01:25,354
taxes and interest.

42
00:01:25,617 --> 00:01:28,048
When Dana's dad started on the loading

43
00:01:28,048 --> 00:01:28,510
dock,

44
00:01:28,510 --> 00:01:30,187
back in the seventies,

45
00:01:30,187 --> 00:01:32,618
workers got about fifty-eight dollars of

46
00:01:32,618 --> 00:01:33,704
that bill.

47
00:01:33,950 --> 00:01:34,667
Today,

48
00:01:34,667 --> 00:01:36,750
they get about fifty-one.

49
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:38,012
And the owners' slice,

50
00:01:38,012 --> 00:01:39,264
profit after tax,

51
00:01:39,264 --> 00:01:40,925
has roughly doubled.

52
00:01:41,184 --> 00:01:44,284
A few dollars doesn't sound like much.

53
00:01:44,284 --> 00:01:45,684
But this bill stands for the whole

54
00:01:45,684 --> 00:01:47,684
American economy.

55
00:01:47,824 --> 00:01:49,023
By our rough math,

56
00:01:49,023 --> 00:01:50,736
that gap is worth more than two

57
00:01:50,736 --> 00:01:52,794
trillion dollars a year.

58
00:01:52,794 --> 00:01:54,074
Not stolen.

59
00:01:54,074 --> 00:01:56,252
Just a split that moved.

60
00:01:56,517 --> 00:01:58,493
The Labor Department tracks the same thing

61
00:01:58,493 --> 00:02:00,477
for private businesses.

62
00:02:00,547 --> 00:02:02,295
For decades after the war,

63
00:02:02,295 --> 00:02:04,457
workers' share barely budged.

64
00:02:04,457 --> 00:02:06,747
Then it started to slide.

65
00:02:06,807 --> 00:02:09,649
Today it's the lowest on record.

66
00:02:09,884 --> 00:02:10,984
Why?

67
00:02:10,984 --> 00:02:13,074
Economists still argue.

68
00:02:13,074 --> 00:02:13,841
Global trade,

69
00:02:13,841 --> 00:02:15,593
a few superstar companies,

70
00:02:15,593 --> 00:02:17,674
and automation all get blamed.

71
00:02:17,674 --> 00:02:19,474
But here's what a robot adds.

72
00:02:19,474 --> 00:02:21,224
A robot is capital.

73
00:02:21,224 --> 00:02:22,769
When a machine does a task a

74
00:02:22,769 --> 00:02:24,102
person used to do,

75
00:02:24,102 --> 00:02:25,841
the money that paid that person now

76
00:02:25,841 --> 00:02:27,986
goes to whoever owns the machine.

77
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:30,650
Dana gets it right away.

78
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:32,657
Her warehouse didn't fire anyone this

79
00:02:32,657 --> 00:02:33,410
year.

80
00:02:33,410 --> 00:02:35,201
It just stopped hiring for the night

81
00:02:35,201 --> 00:02:35,990
shift.

82
00:02:35,990 --> 00:02:37,990
The robots took those hours.

83
00:02:37,990 --> 00:02:39,208
And the money those hours used to

84
00:02:39,208 --> 00:02:40,040
earn didn't vanish.

85
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:41,448
It went somewhere.

86
00:02:45,884 --> 00:02:48,734
So Dana asks the obvious question.

87
00:02:48,734 --> 00:02:50,554
Who owns the machines?

88
00:02:50,554 --> 00:02:51,090
Mostly,

89
00:02:51,090 --> 00:02:52,894
whoever owns the company.

90
00:02:52,894 --> 00:02:53,492
In other words,

91
00:02:53,492 --> 00:02:53,864
stocks.

92
00:02:53,864 --> 00:02:56,281
So let's line up every household in

93
00:02:56,281 --> 00:02:57,357
America,

94
00:02:57,357 --> 00:02:59,312
from the least wealthy to the most,

95
00:02:59,312 --> 00:03:01,290
and share out a hundred dollars' worth

96
00:03:01,290 --> 00:03:02,988
of the stock they own.

97
00:03:03,250 --> 00:03:05,560
The whole bottom half of the country,

98
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:06,598
all together,

99
00:03:06,598 --> 00:03:08,470
gets less than a dollar.

100
00:03:08,717 --> 00:03:11,257
And the richest tenth of households?

101
00:03:11,257 --> 00:03:13,147
They get almost ninety.

102
00:03:13,147 --> 00:03:15,306
Nearly the whole pile.

103
00:03:15,550 --> 00:03:16,913
Dana's neighbor Milo,

104
00:03:16,913 --> 00:03:18,474
a retired bus driver,

105
00:03:18,474 --> 00:03:21,050
has a retirement account from work.

106
00:03:21,190 --> 00:03:23,872
Accounts like his are spread wider than

107
00:03:23,872 --> 00:03:24,740
stocks.

108
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:25,819
But even there,

109
00:03:25,819 --> 00:03:29,062
the poorer households hold only a sliver.

110
00:03:29,317 --> 00:03:31,397
Now connect the dots.

111
00:03:31,397 --> 00:03:33,940
If robots move money from paychecks to

112
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:34,678
profits,

113
00:03:34,678 --> 00:03:36,687
the money climbs this ladder,

114
00:03:36,687 --> 00:03:39,067
to whoever holds the shares.

115
00:03:39,207 --> 00:03:40,397
For generations,

116
00:03:40,397 --> 00:03:43,217
getting ahead meant getting a better job.

117
00:03:43,237 --> 00:03:45,476
When machines do more of the work,

118
00:03:45,476 --> 00:03:47,511
owning a piece of them could matter

119
00:03:47,511 --> 00:03:48,789
just as much.

120
00:03:53,250 --> 00:03:54,192
And right now,

121
00:03:54,192 --> 00:03:57,089
the biggest machines in history are going

122
00:03:57,089 --> 00:03:57,780
up.

123
00:03:57,780 --> 00:03:59,890
Data centers for AI.

124
00:03:59,890 --> 00:04:00,840
Amazon,

125
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:01,791
Alphabet,

126
00:04:01,791 --> 00:04:04,227
Microsoft and Meta spent more than six

127
00:04:04,227 --> 00:04:06,664
hundred billion dollars on buildings and

128
00:04:06,664 --> 00:04:08,589
equipment in the past year,

129
00:04:08,589 --> 00:04:09,920
by our sum.

130
00:04:10,184 --> 00:04:12,115
Much of that money lands at the

131
00:04:12,115 --> 00:04:14,404
company selling the chips.

132
00:04:14,454 --> 00:04:15,928
In its latest quarter,

133
00:04:15,928 --> 00:04:18,278
Nvidia kept most of every dollar of

134
00:04:18,278 --> 00:04:19,947
sales as profit.

135
00:04:20,184 --> 00:04:21,934
Let me draw the loop.

136
00:04:21,934 --> 00:04:24,009
A business pays for AI instead of

137
00:04:24,009 --> 00:04:25,014
hiring.

138
00:04:25,014 --> 00:04:27,454
That money goes to a cloud company.

139
00:04:27,454 --> 00:04:29,552
The cloud company buys chips and builds

140
00:04:29,552 --> 00:04:31,054
data centers.

141
00:04:31,054 --> 00:04:34,543
Profits flow to the shareholders of both.

142
00:04:34,784 --> 00:04:37,654
Now notice who's missing from the loop.

143
00:04:37,654 --> 00:04:40,094
The worker whose task was automated.

144
00:04:40,134 --> 00:04:43,184
Unless that worker also owns shares.

145
00:04:43,450 --> 00:04:45,425
Dana looks at the loop and sees

146
00:04:45,425 --> 00:04:47,210
herself outside it.

147
00:04:47,210 --> 00:04:49,240
Her pay comes from the warehouse.

148
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:51,040
The robots come from someone else's

149
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:51,820
factory,

150
00:04:51,820 --> 00:04:54,220
and they run on someone else's cloud.

151
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:56,890
Every step of that chain has owners.

152
00:04:56,940 --> 00:04:58,680
She isn't one of them.

153
00:04:58,870 --> 00:05:00,004
Yet.

154
00:05:00,250 --> 00:05:01,324
But it's a bet,

155
00:05:01,324 --> 00:05:02,990
not a guarantee.

156
00:05:02,990 --> 00:05:04,682
Some of this spending may never pay

157
00:05:04,682 --> 00:05:05,470
off.

158
00:05:05,510 --> 00:05:06,709
Over the past year,

159
00:05:06,709 --> 00:05:08,944
Amazon spent more cash than its business

160
00:05:08,944 --> 00:05:09,950
brought in.

161
00:05:10,140 --> 00:05:11,800
Owners get the upside.

162
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:14,290
Owners also carry the risk.

163
00:05:18,750 --> 00:05:20,791
So is AI already moving money from

164
00:05:20,791 --> 00:05:23,020
people like Dana to the owners?

165
00:05:23,020 --> 00:05:24,747
Let's separate what's been measured from

166
00:05:24,747 --> 00:05:26,060
what's been predicted.

167
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:26,518
First,

168
00:05:26,518 --> 00:05:27,760
a finding.

169
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:30,115
When a company gave its customer-support

170
00:05:30,115 --> 00:05:31,666
agents an AI assistant,

171
00:05:31,666 --> 00:05:33,412
they solved more problems per hour,

172
00:05:33,412 --> 00:05:34,374
and the newest,

173
00:05:34,374 --> 00:05:37,133
least experienced agents gained the most.

174
00:05:37,384 --> 00:05:38,260
In Denmark,

175
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:40,282
researchers matched chatbot use with

176
00:05:40,282 --> 00:05:42,344
official payroll records.

177
00:05:42,344 --> 00:05:43,961
Since ChatGPT arrived,

178
00:05:43,961 --> 00:05:46,975
they've found no measurable effect on pay

179
00:05:46,975 --> 00:05:48,123
or hours.

180
00:05:48,384 --> 00:05:50,033
But in America,

181
00:05:50,033 --> 00:05:53,024
payroll data shows something to watch.

182
00:05:53,084 --> 00:05:55,158
Among the youngest workers in the jobs

183
00:05:55,158 --> 00:05:56,589
most exposed to AI,

184
00:05:56,589 --> 00:05:58,870
employment is almost a fifth below where

185
00:05:58,870 --> 00:06:00,714
it would otherwise be.

186
00:06:00,804 --> 00:06:03,514
Experienced workers show no such gap.

187
00:06:03,514 --> 00:06:06,200
The researchers call it an early warning,

188
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:07,251
not proof.

189
00:06:07,517 --> 00:06:10,527
Factory robots have a longer record.

190
00:06:10,537 --> 00:06:13,441
Economists found that American towns that

191
00:06:13,441 --> 00:06:15,862
added more robots saw fewer jobs and

192
00:06:15,862 --> 00:06:17,672
slightly lower pay.

193
00:06:17,917 --> 00:06:20,357
Then there are forecasts.

194
00:06:20,357 --> 00:06:23,529
MIT economist Daron Acemoglu predicts AI

195
00:06:23,529 --> 00:06:26,248
will give productivity only a small lift

196
00:06:26,248 --> 00:06:27,812
over the next decade,

197
00:06:27,812 --> 00:06:30,304
and widen the gap between what capital

198
00:06:30,304 --> 00:06:32,037
earns and what work earns.

199
00:06:32,207 --> 00:06:33,147
That's a model,

200
00:06:33,147 --> 00:06:34,897
not a measurement.

201
00:06:35,007 --> 00:06:37,566
Forecasts differ widely.

202
00:06:42,017 --> 00:06:44,767
And this has happened before.

203
00:06:44,767 --> 00:06:45,683
Britain,

204
00:06:45,683 --> 00:06:47,477
the age of steam.

205
00:06:47,477 --> 00:06:49,834
Machines multiplied what every worker

206
00:06:49,834 --> 00:06:50,827
could make.

207
00:06:50,967 --> 00:06:53,107
Factory owners grew rich.

208
00:06:53,107 --> 00:06:55,482
Workers' pay barely moved.

209
00:06:55,717 --> 00:06:58,078
Economic historian Robert Allen measured

210
00:06:58,078 --> 00:06:58,697
it.

211
00:06:58,697 --> 00:07:00,317
Let me draw his story.

212
00:07:00,327 --> 00:07:02,477
Output per worker kept climbing.

213
00:07:02,477 --> 00:07:04,417
Real wages stayed flat.

214
00:07:04,417 --> 00:07:06,227
The profit rate doubled.

215
00:07:06,227 --> 00:07:08,241
Historians call it Engels' pause,

216
00:07:08,241 --> 00:07:09,802
after Friedrich Engels,

217
00:07:09,802 --> 00:07:12,291
who described the factory workers of

218
00:07:12,291 --> 00:07:13,570
Manchester.

219
00:07:13,817 --> 00:07:14,202
Then,

220
00:07:14,202 --> 00:07:16,027
around the middle of the eighteen

221
00:07:16,027 --> 00:07:16,615
hundreds,

222
00:07:16,615 --> 00:07:18,643
wages finally began to rise with

223
00:07:18,643 --> 00:07:20,257
productivity.

224
00:07:20,317 --> 00:07:21,797
The pause ended.

225
00:07:21,797 --> 00:07:23,647
But it lasted decades.

226
00:07:23,647 --> 00:07:25,636
For the workers who lived through it,

227
00:07:25,636 --> 00:07:27,643
eventually came too late.

228
00:07:27,884 --> 00:07:29,993
So are we living through a new

229
00:07:29,993 --> 00:07:31,244
Engels' pause?

230
00:07:31,244 --> 00:07:32,934
Nobody can say yet.

231
00:07:32,934 --> 00:07:35,296
Workers' share of business output is at

232
00:07:35,296 --> 00:07:36,348
a record low,

233
00:07:36,348 --> 00:07:39,524
but AI has barely reached most workplaces.

234
00:07:39,614 --> 00:07:41,559
What history does show is that the

235
00:07:41,559 --> 00:07:43,051
pause wasn't automatic,

236
00:07:43,051 --> 00:07:45,164
and neither was the catch-up.

237
00:07:45,224 --> 00:07:46,724
It depends on new jobs,

238
00:07:46,724 --> 00:07:47,355
new skills,

239
00:07:47,355 --> 00:07:48,877
and who gets to own the new

240
00:07:48,877 --> 00:07:50,129
machines.

241
00:07:54,584 --> 00:07:55,064
Now,

242
00:07:55,064 --> 00:07:56,624
the other side.

243
00:07:56,624 --> 00:07:56,829
First,

244
00:07:56,829 --> 00:07:57,244
new jobs.

245
00:07:57,244 --> 00:07:59,910
A team led by economist David Autor

246
00:07:59,910 --> 00:08:02,354
found that most Americans today work in

247
00:08:02,354 --> 00:08:04,353
job specialties that didn't exist in

248
00:08:04,353 --> 00:08:05,904
nineteen forty.

249
00:08:05,904 --> 00:08:08,014
Machines take over tasks.

250
00:08:08,014 --> 00:08:10,515
New kinds of work keep appearing.

251
00:08:10,750 --> 00:08:11,359
Second,

252
00:08:11,359 --> 00:08:13,640
productivity can lift pay.

253
00:08:13,640 --> 00:08:15,260
When things get cheaper to make,

254
00:08:15,260 --> 00:08:16,640
people buy more.

255
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:18,354
And workers who use AI well can

256
00:08:18,354 --> 00:08:20,190
become more valuable.

257
00:08:20,190 --> 00:08:20,958
Like Dana,

258
00:08:20,958 --> 00:08:22,936
who now runs the robot fleet.

259
00:08:23,184 --> 00:08:23,370
Third,

260
00:08:23,370 --> 00:08:24,104
skills still pay.

261
00:08:24,104 --> 00:08:26,639
A full-time worker with at least a

262
00:08:26,639 --> 00:08:30,018
bachelor's degree typically earns about

263
00:08:30,018 --> 00:08:32,271
three-quarters more than a high school

264
00:08:32,271 --> 00:08:33,434
graduate.

265
00:08:33,684 --> 00:08:34,627
And fourth,

266
00:08:34,627 --> 00:08:37,504
policy can change the split.

267
00:08:37,574 --> 00:08:39,436
Some want to spread ownership wider,

268
00:08:39,436 --> 00:08:40,734
with employee ownership,

269
00:08:40,734 --> 00:08:41,656
accounts for kids,

270
00:08:41,656 --> 00:08:43,349
or public funds like Alaska's oil

271
00:08:43,349 --> 00:08:44,083
dividend,

272
00:08:44,083 --> 00:08:46,384
which we covered in another film.

273
00:08:46,384 --> 00:08:47,574
Others prefer training,

274
00:08:47,574 --> 00:08:49,299
or changing how work and capital are

275
00:08:49,299 --> 00:08:49,984
taxed.

276
00:08:49,984 --> 00:08:52,734
Others say markets will sort it out.

277
00:08:52,734 --> 00:08:55,124
Each has trade-offs.

278
00:08:55,184 --> 00:08:57,061
This film takes no side.

279
00:09:01,517 --> 00:09:04,567
So Dana asks the practical question.

280
00:09:04,617 --> 00:09:07,407
What can an ordinary person actually own?

281
00:09:07,407 --> 00:09:08,337
Quick note.

282
00:09:08,337 --> 00:09:09,562
This is education,

283
00:09:09,562 --> 00:09:11,067
not investment advice.

284
00:09:11,067 --> 00:09:13,287
We're not telling anyone what to buy.

285
00:09:13,327 --> 00:09:15,576
Most American families own at least some

286
00:09:15,576 --> 00:09:15,933
stock,

287
00:09:15,933 --> 00:09:18,407
often through a retirement account.

288
00:09:18,467 --> 00:09:20,690
But among lower-income families,

289
00:09:20,690 --> 00:09:22,717
fewer than one in three do.

290
00:09:22,984 --> 00:09:24,824
Let me draw the doors.

291
00:09:24,824 --> 00:09:27,155
The most common is a workplace retirement

292
00:09:27,155 --> 00:09:27,718
account,

293
00:09:27,718 --> 00:09:29,394
like a four-oh-one-k.

294
00:09:29,394 --> 00:09:30,050
Inside,

295
00:09:30,050 --> 00:09:32,065
many people hold index funds,

296
00:09:32,065 --> 00:09:34,102
which own small slices of hundreds of

297
00:09:34,102 --> 00:09:35,834
companies at once.

298
00:09:35,864 --> 00:09:38,234
Some employers offer stock plans.

299
00:09:38,234 --> 00:09:40,655
Employee stock ownership plans alone cover

300
00:09:40,655 --> 00:09:42,356
millions of people.

301
00:09:42,617 --> 00:09:45,217
But owning isn't free money.

302
00:09:45,217 --> 00:09:47,432
Stock prices can fall hard and take

303
00:09:47,432 --> 00:09:49,417
years to recover.

304
00:09:49,437 --> 00:09:51,587
Fees eat returns.

305
00:09:51,607 --> 00:09:53,807
And betting your savings on your own

306
00:09:53,807 --> 00:09:54,515
employer,

307
00:09:54,515 --> 00:09:56,935
or on a single hot company,

308
00:09:56,935 --> 00:09:58,757
piles risk on risk.

309
00:09:58,917 --> 00:10:01,416
Owning a slice of the machines means

310
00:10:01,416 --> 00:10:03,515
sharing their bad years too.

311
00:10:03,750 --> 00:10:04,770
As for Dana,

312
00:10:04,770 --> 00:10:07,061
she finally reads the benefits papers

313
00:10:07,061 --> 00:10:08,830
she'd ignored for years.

314
00:10:08,830 --> 00:10:10,889
Her employer offers a retirement plan,

315
00:10:10,889 --> 00:10:12,590
and she never signed up.

316
00:10:12,590 --> 00:10:13,596
That's her decision,

317
00:10:13,596 --> 00:10:15,170
not advice for anyone else.

318
00:10:15,170 --> 00:10:17,265
But it's the moment she realizes she

319
00:10:17,265 --> 00:10:18,941
could stand on both sides of the

320
00:10:18,941 --> 00:10:19,670
loop.

321
00:10:19,670 --> 00:10:20,799
Earning a paycheck,

322
00:10:20,799 --> 00:10:22,330
and owning a small slice of the

323
00:10:22,330 --> 00:10:23,504
machines.

324
00:10:27,950 --> 00:10:28,379
So,

325
00:10:28,379 --> 00:10:30,184
who gets rich when robots do the

326
00:10:30,184 --> 00:10:31,010
work?

327
00:10:31,090 --> 00:10:32,521
On the evidence so far,

328
00:10:32,521 --> 00:10:34,530
mostly the people who own them.

329
00:10:34,530 --> 00:10:36,559
The shareholders of the companies that

330
00:10:36,559 --> 00:10:36,909
build,

331
00:10:36,909 --> 00:10:38,770
sell and run the machines.

332
00:10:38,770 --> 00:10:40,021
And in America,

333
00:10:40,021 --> 00:10:41,471
that's a group where the richest tenth

334
00:10:41,471 --> 00:10:43,522
hold nearly the whole pile.

335
00:10:43,784 --> 00:10:45,728
But it doesn't have to stay that

336
00:10:45,728 --> 00:10:46,884
narrow.

337
00:10:46,984 --> 00:10:48,770
History says pay can catch up,

338
00:10:48,770 --> 00:10:50,374
after a delay.

339
00:10:50,584 --> 00:10:52,474
New jobs do appear.

340
00:10:52,474 --> 00:10:54,844
And ownership isn't a closed club.

341
00:10:54,984 --> 00:10:57,157
more than half of American families

342
00:10:57,157 --> 00:10:58,520
already own some stock,

343
00:10:58,520 --> 00:11:00,898
even if most own very little.

344
00:11:01,150 --> 00:11:02,400
For generations,

345
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:04,075
the big money question was,

346
00:11:04,075 --> 00:11:06,370
what do you do for a living?

347
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:07,660
Dana's question now has a second part.

348
00:11:07,660 --> 00:11:09,576
What do you own?

349
00:11:09,817 --> 00:11:10,424
Next time,

350
00:11:10,424 --> 00:11:13,143
we follow the money behind another hidden

351
00:11:13,143 --> 00:11:15,407
link of the world economy.

352
00:11:15,407 --> 00:11:18,150
This is The Hidden Economics.
